<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Results (%) comparison between StruSP and its ablative variants. RAGCL is the backbone model. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the effectiveness of StruSP, we conduct an ablation study of four ablative versions by removing structure-aware hybrid sampling(<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o SHS), masked propagation modeling (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o MPM), bidirectional evolutionary propagation learning strategy (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o BEP), and structure-aware propagation enhancement (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o SPE). The results are shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Structure-aware Propagation Generation with Large Language Models for Fake News Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. It can be observed that the full StruSP <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/RAGCL achieves better performance on both datasets. The performance drop of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o SHS on both datasets demonstrates the importance of modeling bidirectional evolutionary propagation. Similarly, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o MPM shows degraded results, indicating that providing LLMs with contextual propagation paths aids in capturing propagation dynamics. And the removal of both modules (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o BEP) leads to the most significant decline, confirming that the two components are complementary and jointly crucial for realistic propagation generation. Furthermore, removing the structure-aware propagation enhancement module (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>/o SPE) results in a noticeable performance drop, indicating that generating propagation solely from news content is less effective. This confirms that leveraging partial real propagation as guidance leads to more informative and structurally aligned synthetic propagation for detection.</p>
</div>
